-*- coding:utf-8 -*-

alfe-workdir-env-deletes-caller-directory — $AUTOLISP_WORKDIR is taken as alfe's own workdir and removed at exit
================================================================================================================

Reported from: SCHMS+ test harness (sncf-reseau/src/schms, Makefile
targets test-cad-* / test-unitaire-clautolisp) driving
`alfe --bricscad --mode batch` on macOS, BricsCAD V26.

Observed with the installed alfe 1.6.0. The code path is unchanged at
repository HEAD (alfe 1.6.14).

Summary
-------

`$AUTOLISP_WORKDIR` is read as an override for **alfe's own protocol
workdir** (`autolisp-front-end/source/cli.lisp:181`, mapping
`:workdir` -> `"AUTOLISP_WORKDIR"`; documented at cli.lisp:249 as
"--workdir DIR  Override $AUTOLISP_WORKDIR"). At the end of the run,
`alfe.workdir:remove-workdir`
(`autolisp-front-end/source/workdir.lisp:84`) does
`uiop:delete-directory-tree` on it unless `--keep-workdir` /
`$AUTOLISP_KEEP_WORKDIR` is set.

So a caller that exports `AUTOLISP_WORKDIR` pointing at one of its own
directories has that directory **recursively deleted**, silently, when
alfe exits.

How it bit us
-------------

`AUTOLISP_WORKDIR` is also the name the AutoLISP-side test harnesses use
for "where to write my artefacts" — it is the variable the SCHMS+ harness
reads (`tu:workdir`) to place `errors.log`, and the same name appears in
the CAD probe conventions. Exporting it before invoking alfe is the
obvious thing to do:

    AUTOLISP_WORKDIR="$build/cad" \
    alfe -norc --bricscad --mode batch -l tests/test-cad.lsp -x '(C:MAIN)'

Result: the suite runs and passes, then alfe deletes `$build/cad`
including the `.out`, `.err` and `.status` files the harness had just
written. The calling Makefile then reports

    Tests cad failed under BricsCAD: status=missing

for a run that was in fact fully green — an outcome that looks like a
harness bug and costs real time to trace back to alfe.

Expected
--------

At least one of, in decreasing order of preference:

1. Never delete a workdir that alfe did not create. `remove-workdir`
   should only remove auto-generated workdirs (the
   `alfe-<backend>-<pid>-<hash>` ones); a directory supplied by the user
   via `--workdir` or `$AUTOLISP_WORKDIR` should be cleaned of alfe's own
   protocol files at most, never `delete-directory-tree`'d.

2. Failing that, refuse to use a non-empty caller-supplied directory, or
   create a private subdirectory inside it and delete only that.

3. At minimum: document the destructive behaviour loudly in `--help` and
   in the workdir documentation ("alfe DELETES this directory on exit"),
   since the current wording "Override $AUTOLISP_WORKDIR" gives no hint.

A separate name for the two concepts would also help: alfe's protocol
scratch directory and the AutoLISP program's output directory are not the
same thing, yet they currently share one environment variable.

Workaround in use
-----------------

Do not export `AUTOLISP_WORKDIR`; pass the harness directory in-band
instead, which alfe does not interpret:

    alfe … -x '(setq *AUTOLISP_WORKDIR* "<dir>")' -l … -x '(C:MAIN)'

(see the RUN_TESTS_WITH_ALFE macro in sncf-reseau/src/schms/Makefile,
which carries a comment to that effect so nobody re-adds the export).

Severity
--------

Data loss. It is recursive deletion of a user-designated directory,
triggered by setting a documented environment variable to a plausible
value.
